-lineheight:125%;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f4e2106-1742-4edd-a195-a474795b8511-attributes-textshadow:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f4e2106-1742-4edd-a195-a474795b8511-attributes-textstroke:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f4e2106-1742-4edd-a195-a474795b8511-attributes-letterspacing:0px;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f4e2106-1742-4edd-a195-a474795b8511-attributes-texttransform:undefined"><span style="width:100%"><p><span style="font-weight: normal;">"Deficiencies in key micronutrients, including magnesium, zinc, calcium, and vitamin D, are associated</span><b> with increased severity of period pain.</b>"</p></span></div><div data-rid="021fac64-8162-4dca-8759-be47b64a5630" class="r-11bqdlp alchemy-rte" style="--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-color:#2A2A2AFF;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-fontsize:8px;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-textdecoration:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-textalign:left;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-fontfamily:Quicksand;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-fontweight:400;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-lineheight:125%;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-textshadow:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-textstroke:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-letterspacing:0px;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-71e50621-f0f3-4a8c-9b18-55b8dd211253-attributes-texttransform:undefined"><span style="width:100%"><p><strong>Source:</strong>&nbsp;A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis (PMC, 2020).</p></span></div></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="5ba57532-380c-436f-87a4-a0600532abf4" class="r-1rr3slh alchemy-rte" style="--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-color:#2A2A2A;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-fontsize:17px;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-textdecoration:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-textalign:center;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-fontfamily:Quicksand;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-fontweight:400;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-lineheight:125%;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-textshadow:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-textstroke:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-letterspacing:0px;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-e2bf30c6-7292-47b6-96b1-1bb9a4ff7feb-attributes-texttransform:undefined"><span style="width:100%"><p>Painkillers only target the second trigger behind period pain. That’s why they don’t always provide full relief for intense cramps —and for some, they don’t work at all.*<br><br><br>Yet, many women find themselves taking more than they’d like just to get through their period, despite the fact that even light use is known to cause gut and gastrointestinal issues.*<br><br><br>The problem? Painkillers can't fix your hormones, and they definitely don’t fill nutrient gaps. That’s why the pain keeps coming back just as strong every month — along with the bloating, fatigue, and mood swings that make periods even harder.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<br><br><strong><u>But here's the good news:<br></u></strong><br><br>Leading specialists in menstrual health have found that by tackling all three underlying triggers at once, you can get ahead of cramps before they start — becoming the woman who barely notices them and doesn’t have to battle PMS or other monthly symptoms— thanks to a breakthrough approach.</p></span></div><div data-rid="746eed23-6466-4d48-9b75-fbd5056aacb4" tabindex="0" role="link" class="r-1q1ozuk alchemy-rte" style="--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f775258-2b0d-43d7-b73f-26639a2f7dc0-attributes-color:#2A2A2A;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f775258-2b0d-43d7-b73f-26639a2f7dc0-attributes-fontsize:12px;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f775258-2b0d-43d7-b73f-26639a2f7dc0-attributes-textdecoration:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f775258-2b0d-43d7-b73f-26639a2f7dc0-attributes-fontfamily:Quicksand;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f775258-2b0d-43d7-b73f-26639a2f7dc0-attributes-fontweight:700;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f775258-2b0d-43d7-b73f-26639a2f7dc0-attributes-lineheight:16px;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f775258-2b0d-43d7-b73f-26639a2f7dc0-attributes-textshadow:undefined;--replo-library-7229a9be-0ff2-4194-85c2-a9c88cbfa244-styles-9f